{
  "raw": {
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Adjunct-Instructor_JR102551": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nDepartment: Criminal Justice & Legal Studies Job Summary: The Department of Criminal Justice & Legal Studies at The University of Mississippi invites applications for Adjunct Instructors. Selected applicants will join a pool of instructors for future part-time teaching appointments when a course opening is available, and the applicant’s qualifications match departmental instructional needs. Adjunct Instructors focus on teaching in their areas of academic, professional, or practitioner expertise in criminal justice, legal studies, and related fields. Job Description: The duties and responsibilities of the position include, but are not limited to: Instruct undergraduate and/or graduate students in assigned courses. Develop and maintain course materials, learning activities, assignments, and assessments to achieve course learning outcomes. Deliver instruction in face-to-face, online, hybrid, or other approved formats. Evaluate student performance and provide timely feedback. Maintain accurate course records and submit grades and other required materials by established deadlines. Communicate with students in a timely and professional manner. Be familiar with university-approved learning management systems and instructional technologies. Faculty assigned to teach online courses must complete required University of Mississippi Blackboard teaching training prior to instruction. Follow department, school, and university policies and expectations. Other duties as determined by the Department of Criminal Justice & Legal Studies. Minimum Qualifications Master's degree with at least 18 graduate credit hours from an accredited college or university in criminal justice, criminology, legal studies, law, public administration, sociology, psychology, political science, or a closely related field. Preferred Qualifications Previous teaching experience at the college or university level. Experience teaching in online and/or hybrid learning environments. Professional experience in criminal justice, legal studies, law, corrections, courts, law enforcement, victim services, emergency management, homeland security, cybersecurity, or related fields and/or a terminal degree (Ph.D., J.D., or other relevant terminal degree).  Experience using learning management systems and instructional technologies. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Education Qualifications: Master's, Master's: Criminal Justice (Required), MCJ Experience: Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Instructional-Assistant-Professor_JR103386": {
      "title": "Instructional Assistant Professor of Italian",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nDepartment: The Department of Modern Languages at the University of Mississippi [modernlanguages.olemiss.edu] seeks an Instructional Assistant Professor of Italian to begin January 2027. The successful candidate will teach 9-12 hours per semester, mainly at the elementary and intermediate level in regular and intensive formats (6 credits each), but also upper-level culture courses. The successful candidate must hold a Ph.D. in Italian or a related field, have native or near-native proficiency in Italian, and demonstrate solid teaching experience. At the same time, this person will also engage in student recruitment to and promotion of the Italian program. While the position is non-tenure-track, it is renewable annually with the possibility of promotions.   Interested candidates should visit [https://careers.olemiss.edu] to complete an application by uploading a cover letter and a CV listing the names and contact information for three references. Inquiries may be made to Dr. Daniel E. O’Sullivan, Professor of French and Chair of Modern Languages (dosulliv@olemiss.edu). Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled or an adequate applicant pool is established. The University of Mississippi is an EEO/AA/Title VI/Title IX/Section 504/ADA/ADEA employer.   Job Summary: Primarily performs a limited range of duties. And are usually assigned teaching or service roles. Not eligible for tenure. Job Description: Faculty members in this category are hired primarily to perform a limited range of duties. Non-Tenure Track Faculty members are usually assigned teaching or service roles. Non-Tenure Track Faculty members may contribute to the University beyond this narrow definition, but their promotion guidelines and annual reviews should emphasize the principal duties. Certain faculty with professorial titles in these categories can be permanent employees and can expect to be offered annual contracts, unless termination notices are provided in a timely manner as outlined in the Termination of Non-Tenured Faculty Policy. Visiting Faculty, Faculty Fellows, Adjunct Faculty, or Artists/Writers-in-Residence are generally considered temporary employees. For these latter appointments, the employment period must be specified in the employment notification and renewal of their appointments is at the discretion of the University. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Teach Italian language classes at all levels Education Qualifications: PhD: Italian (Required) Experience: Related Experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Assistant-Professor_JR103408": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Biology Job Summary: Tenure Track Faculty members perform the functions of teaching, research or creative activities, and service and are assigned to an academic unit appropriate for their Discipline descriptor. Tenure Track Faculty members are usually hired as assistant professors and enter the tenure track. For example, an assistant professor must serve a probationary period of five years and be reviewed for tenure during his or her sixth year of continuous employment at the University. Tenure Track Faculty members may be hired, particularly at the associate and full ranks, with a reduction in the tenure probationary period, as explained in the Tenure Policy. Tenure Track Faculty members at the Associate and Full ranks are full members of the Graduate Faculty, while those at the Assistant rank are Associate Members of the Graduate Faculty. Job Description: The Department of Biology at the University of Mississippi invites applications for a 9-month tenure-track Assistant Professor position in the broad area of Quantitative Cell Biology. We seek candidates who use data-rich experimental approaches to study cells and to uncover molecular mechanisms across cellular, developmental, microbial, plant, organismal, and ecological systems. We are especially interested in candidates with strong expertise in experimental approaches for measuring gene expression, gene regulation, and dynamic biological processes in living cells or multicellular organisms, who are prepared to lead an independent research program driven by fundamental science and curiosity-driven questions. Preferred approaches include quantitative live-cell imaging, optogenetics, advanced bioimage analysis, spatial and temporal gene expression measurements, phenomics, and AI-enabled analysis of biological data. This position should build upon the department’s existing strengths while significantly expanding its capacity in quantitative, mechanistic, and the integration of biology across scales and methods. The Department of Biology is a broad-based department of 25 tenure-track and 21 instructional faculty members that educates over 900 undergraduate biology majors and more than 60 graduate students (Ph.D. and M.S.). More information about the department can be found at https://biology.olemiss.edu/. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. from an accredited university in Biology, Biophysics, or a related field. Postdoctoral or other post-Ph.D. research experience relevant to the position (e.g., research assistant professor, non-academic research position). For consideration, your application must include: A cover letter outlining interest and suitability for the position; Curriculum vitae; A 2-3 page statement of research interests, outlining ongoing and proposed research areas; A 1-2 page teaching statement, which includes teaching experience and teaching interests; Names and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue through January 15, 2027 . The desired starting date is August 2027 . Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Performs the functions of teaching, research or creative activities, and service and are assigned to an academic unit appropriate for their Discipline descriptor. Education Qualifications: PhD (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Professor-of-Practice---Manufacturing-Extension-Program-Director_JR103405": {
      "title": "Professor of Practice & Manufacturing Extension Program Director",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nDepartment: The Center for Manufacturing Excellence's (CME) Job Summary: Performs duties in instruction, including the teaching of practical skills and demonstrating best professional practices. Not eligible for tenure. Job Description: The Professor of Practice & Manufacturing Extension Program Director provides leadership for the Center for Manufacturing Excellence's (CME) manufacturing extension and industry engagement activities. The position connects University expertise, educational resources, faculty, and students with manufacturers throughout Mississippi and the region to strengthen organizational performance, workforce capability, operational excellence, and competitiveness. As a Professor of Practice, the individual also contributes to CME's academic mission through teaching, applied scholarship, student engagement, and curriculum development. The position is practice oriented and is not expected to conduct traditional academic research. The rank of the Professor of Practice appointment will be determined based on the selected candidate’s qualifications and relevant professional experience. The Program Director develops, leads, and grows a comprehensive manufacturing extension program responsive to the needs of manufacturers and other industry partners. Working collaboratively with CME faculty and staff, industry, and economic and workforce development partners, the incumbent identifies industry needs and develops training, technical assistance, professional education, applied projects, and other services to address those needs. The position will emphasize industry engagement, operational excellence and continuous improvement, applied problem solving, external funding, professional and graduate education, and measurable extension impact. The Program Director will also teach graduate and undergraduate courses consistent with the individual's expertise and support CME's Graduate Certificate in Operational Excellence and related academic and professional programs. Examples of Work Performed Provide strategic and operational leadership for CME's manufacturing extension program and develop a sustainable structure and portfolio of industry-facing services. Build and maintain relationships with manufacturers, industry associations, economic development organizations, workforce development agencies, and other external partners. Identify manufacturing needs and develop customized or scalable training, technical assistance, and applied problem-solving initiatives. Develop and deliver programs in areas such as Lean manufacturing, Six Sigma, continuous improvement, operational excellence, project management, manufacturing leadership, supply chain, and operations strategy, consistent with the individual's expertise. Coordinate CME faculty, staff, students, University resources, and external subject-matter experts in support of industry projects and extension activities. Teach CME graduate and undergraduate courses and contribute to curriculum development to include online coursework and course/program assessment. Mentor and engage students in industry sponsored projects, applied scholarship, internships, and other experiential learning activities. Produce and disseminate practice oriented applied scholarship, including industry case studies, technical reports, professional presentations, instructional materials, and best practice resources. Identify and pursue grants, contracts, sponsorships, fee-based programming, and other external funding to support and expand manufacturing extension activities. Establish measures of program effectiveness and communicate extension outcomes and impact to industry partners, University leadership, funding organizations, and other stakeholders. Represent CME and the University at manufacturing conferences, industry meetings, professional organizations, and economic development activities. Identify emerging manufacturing trends, technologies, workforce challenges, and practices and translate them into new CME programs, services, and educational opportunities. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: . Education Qualifications: PhD (Required) Experience: Project Management Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/American-Sign-Language-Interpreting-Instructor_JR103375": {
      "title": "American Sign Language Interpreting Instructor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Communication Sciences and Disorders Job Summary: The Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders at the University of Mississippi invites applications for an American Sign Language/Interpreting Instructor. This is a nine-month, non-tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Instructor. Applicants must hold a master's degree, or its equivalent, in American Sign Language instruction, interpreting, Deaf Studies, or a closely related field. Preference will be given to candidates who demonstrate native or near-native proficiency in American Sign Language. Previous teaching experience, particularly at the postsecondary level, professional interpreting experience, and state-level or national interpreter certification are preferred. Job Description: The successful candidate will teach courses in the American Sign Language Interpreting Minor program on the Oxford, Mississippi campus. Candidates should demonstrate knowledge of Deaf culture and the cultural and linguistic foundations of American Sign Language, as well as an understanding of professional and ethical practices relevant to ASL instruction and interpreting. The successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate American Sign Language courses, prepare students to communicate effectively and appropriately with ASL users, and to work professionally with individuals and communities they may encounter in interpreting settings. Additional responsibilities may include contributing to curriculum development and program activities, advising and mentoring students, participating in departmental and university service, and maintaining current knowledge of professional standards and practices in American Sign Language instruction and interpreting. Review of applications will begin immediately. The position will remain open until filled or until an adequate applicant pool is established. The start date is negotiable. Applicants must apply online at https://careers.olemiss.edu and must include a letter of application, curriculum vitae, and the contact information of three professional references; no paper copies will be accepted. For additional information about this position, please contact Ronda Bryan (rbryan@olemiss.edu). Official transcripts must be received before hire. The University of Mississippi is an EOE/AA / Minorities / Females / Vet / Disability / Sexual Orientation / Gender Identity / Title VI / Title VII / Title IX/504 / ADA / ADEA employer. The University of Mississippi is a member of the Southeastern Conference and is included in the elite group of Doctoral Universities – R1, or Highest Research Activity by the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education. Over the last 12 years, the University of Mississippi has been continuously recognized as one of the best colleges in the nation to work for by The Chronicle of Higher Education. The School of Applied Sciences, located on the University of Mississippi campus in Oxford, MS (https://olemiss.edu/), is an inclusive home to approximately 150 faculty and staff, over 2,600 students, six academic departments, four centers and an institute. The School currently serves 10 undergraduate, 10 master’s and 4 doctoral programs (https://sas.olemiss.edu/). The Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders at the University of Mississippi vision includes an exemplary educational experience that prepares the next generation of clinicians, researchers, scholars, and leaders in the field of communication sciences and disorders. The American Sign Language Interpreting Minor is designed to prepare students to pursue state level interpreter credentialing. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Persons with the rank of instructor shall be hired for teaching duties but shall not be tenure track. The contracts will be renewable at the discretion of the University. The individual chosen will perform the function of teaching with renewable contracts. Not eligible for tenure. Education Qualifications: Master's: American Sign Language Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Instructional-Assistant-Professor-of-Finance_JR103374": {
      "title": "Instructional Assistant Professor of Finance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Founded in 1848, the University of Mississippi (UM), affectionately known to alumni, students and friends as Ole Miss, is Mississippi's flagship university. Included in the elite group of R-1: Doctoral Universities - Highest Research Activity by the Carnegie Classification, it has a long history of producing leaders in public service, academics, and business. Located in Oxford, MS, an exceptionally livable upscale college town (www.oxfordms.com), Ole Miss is nestled in the North Mississippi hill country, a one-hour drive south of Memphis, TN. Ole Miss was named the country’s most beautiful campus by USA Today , the #1 college town to retire in by Kiplinger’s , and the #1 food town in the South by Southern Living . Oxford is also known as the home of Nobel and Pulitzer Prize winning author William Faulkner and been featured as a literary and arts destination in such publications as The New York Times , Condé Nast Traveler , and GQ . Among other cultural activities, annual events include the Double Decker Arts Festival, the Oxford Film Festival, a thriving local music scene, and the Ford Center Performing Arts Series. The University of Mississippi has been named in the Chronicle of Higher Education’s “Great Colleges to Work For” list for the last 12 consecutive years out of the list’s 13-year history. Salary for this position is competitive, while the cost of living in Oxford is relatively low. Job Summary: The Finance Department of the School of Business Administration at The University of Mississippi (Ole Miss) invites applications for a non-tenure-track faculty position in Finance at our main campus in Oxford, Mississippi. This position is open at the rank of Instructional Assistant Professor of Finance. Job Description: The successful applicant will be responsible for teaching undergraduate and/or MBA-level courses in Finance, with a specific focus on Economic Statistics. Because our programs serve a diverse and growing student body, the teaching load may require a mix of both online and in-person instruction. The teaching load is typically 3-4 courses in each of the fall and spring semesters. Primary responsibilities include delivering high-quality, rigorous instruction that promotes student success, mentoring students, and performing duties assigned by the Dean of the School of Business Administration and the Chair of the Department of Finance. The successful applicant is expected to maintain Scholarly Active status, as defined by AACSB standards and School policy, at all times. The applicant is also expected to participate in the faculty governance of the School through service responsibilities assigned by the department chair and the dean. Options to teach summer courses for additional pay may be available subject to course and funding availability. Well qualified applicants will credibly be able to teach Economic Statistics and related finance courses. Some relevant industry experience is preferred. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Teach a 3-4 course load in the fall and springs semesters, and to perform duties assigned by the Dean of the School of Business Administration and Chair of the Department of Finance. Education Qualifications: PhD (Required) Experience: Related Experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Instructional-Assistant-Associate-Professor-of-Athletic-Training_JR103345": {
      "title": "Instructional Assistant/Associate Professor of Athletic Training",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Kinesiology and Sport (formerly Health, Exercise Science & Recreation Management) Job Summary: Primarily performs a limited range of duties. And are usually assigned teaching or service roles. Not eligible for tenure. Seeking two full-time, 9-month, non-tenure-track instructional faculty positions in the CAATE-accredited Master of Science in Athletic Training (MSAT) program. Provides graduate-level didactic instruction, mentors students through clinical education, and engages in service. One or both hires may be asked to assume Program Director and/or Director of Clinical Education (Clinical Education Coordinator) administrative responsibilities. Assistant or Associate level is dependent upon qualifications and experience. Terminal degrees in a related field will be considered (e.g. PhD, EdD, DAT, etc.) Job Description: Faculty members in this category are hired primarily to perform a limited range of duties. Non-Tenure Track Faculty members are usually assigned teaching or service roles. Non-Tenure Track Faculty members may contribute to the University beyond this narrow definition, but their promotion guidelines and annual reviews should emphasize the principal duties. Certain faculty with professorial titles in these categories can be permanent employees and can expect to be offered annual contracts, unless termination notices are provided in a timely manner as outlined in the Termination of Non-Tenured Faculty Policy. Visiting Faculty, Faculty Fellows, Adjunct Faculty, or Artists/Writers-in-Residence are generally considered temporary employees. For these latter appointments, the employment period must be specified in the employment notification and renewal of their appointments is at the discretion of the University. Assistant or Associate level dependent upon qualifications and experience. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Please submit a letter of interest (indicating teaching/content expertise and interest in the Program Director and/or Director of Clinical Education role), current CV, statement of teaching philosophy, three professional references, and unofficial graduate transcripts. Education Qualifications: EdD, PhD: Athletic Training (Required) Experience: Related Experience, Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Assistant-Professor_JR103346": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Public Health Job Summary: The Department of Public Health (est. 2024) housed in the School of Applied Sciences at the University of Mississippi is seeking applications for an Assistant Professor. This is a 9-month tenure-track position to begin as early as January 2027. Applicants should have expertise in epidemiology as the responsibilities include teaching undergraduate and graduate public health courses in epidemiology. Applicants should demonstrate a record of, or strong potential for, an active and sustainable research program. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to the Department’s mission, participate in service to the Department, School, University, and/or community, and collaborate with faculty and external partners as appropriate. Job Description: The Department of Public Health (est. 2024) housed in the School of Applied Sciences at the University of Mississippi is seeking applications for an Assistant Professor. This is a 9-month tenure-track position to begin as early as January 2027. Applicants should have expertise in epidemiology as the responsibilities include teaching undergraduate and graduate public health courses in epidemiology. Applicants should demonstrate a record of, or strong potential for, an active and sustainable research program. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to the Department’s mission, participate in service to the Department, School, University, and/or community, and collaborate with faculty and external partners as appropriate. Minimum Qualifications: Doctoral degree in public health or a health-related field by the time of employment Master’s degree in public health (MPH) or a health-related field Evidence of an active research agenda in public health, epidemiology, or a related area, with qualifications and scholarly accomplishments consistent with rank. Evidence of the ability or potential to secure competitive extramural research funding. Applicants must be authorized to work in the US on a full-time basis without the need for visa sponsorship. Preferred Qualifications : General epidemiologist with coursework in infectious diseases, but capable of teaching most undergraduate or MPH courses History of developing or working with partnerships with epidemiological agencies on the local, state, or federal level and involvement in professional organizations Experience developing and teaching online and hybrid courses Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: 40% Teaching in public health 40% Research in public health 20% service to department, SAS, university and field of public health Education Qualifications: PhD Experience: Related Experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Assistant-Professor-of-Practice-in-Documentary-Expression_JR103293": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Practice in Documentary Expression",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nDepartment: About The Center for the Study of Southern Culture Founded in 1977, the CSSC defines its mission as investigating, documenting, interpreting, and teaching about the U.S. South through academic inquiry and publications, documentary studies of film, photography, and oral history, and public outreach programs. The cross-disciplinary Southern Studies faculty and the undergraduate and graduate degree programs (MA and MFA) are the core of the Center’s work. Its curricula incorporate traditional disciplines of the arts, humanities, and social sciences to form an interdisciplinary framework for studying the South. The Center promotes research on the region’s varied cultures by supporting the work of its faculty, staff, students, alumni, and visiting scholars. For more information: https://olemiss.edu/southernstudies/ About Oxford and the University of Mississippi Mississippi provides a rich environment for research opportunities in the humanities. The university is located in Oxford, which boasts a vibrant literary, musical, and arts-focused community and has been consistently ranked as one of the best college towns in the U.S. (In 2021, ESPN named Oxford the single best college town in America.) https://livability.com/ms/oxford Founded in 1848, the University of Mississippi is the state's flagship university and is ranked among the nation's fastest-growing institutions. The Chronicle of Higher Education lists UM as one of the best colleges to work for in the U.S. The university has an R-1 Highest Research Activity Carnegie Classification and a student body of approximately 24,000 students, including 24% students of color and 4% international students from over 80 countries. Job Summary: The Southern Documentary Project (southdocs.org) is looking for an Assistant Professor of Practice in Documentary Expression with a specific interest in documenting African American experiences. We welcome applications from critical storytellers whose creative work centers on Black American and African Diasporic cultures, histories, and identities through a lens of informed practices and ethics. We encourage makers who honor lived experiences and models of cocreation in their media practice with a demonstrated understanding of the Black media canon. SouthDocs is an institute of the Center for the Study of Southern Culture (https://olemiss.edu/southernstudies/) whose mission is to tell stories from the U.S. South, increase civic and cultural engagement, and train the next generation of storytellers enrolled in the Center’s academic programs. In addition to teaching and mentoring students, this person will actively contribute to the Center’s mission through creating original and collaborative work. This permanent nine-month, non-tenure track academic position includes a 2+2 teaching load with a clear path for advancement and promotion. What We Value With this hire, SouthDocs hopes to deepen our commitment to the diversity of our region. As part of an institution with a difficult history in regards to access, we take seriously our unique obligation to educate and lead the state with a commitment toward the goal of complicating our shared narratives. Job Description: 2+2 teaching load with advising responsibilities Work directly with students in the classroom and in the field. See examples of student work at https://southdocs.org/showcase/ Create original documentary projects (individually and collaboratively) utilizing photography, audio, oral history, video, or mixed media. See examples of our work at http://southdocs.org/ . Ensure accountability for and ethical representation of people and places you choose to document. Design and present technical and theory-based workshops or classes and provide constructive feedback related to documentary storytelling and related media. Engage community partners and build long-term relationships through impact-driven storytelling and programs. See https://southdocs.org/project/movementstartshere/ for one recent SouthDocs example and https://www.southernfoodways.org/features/the-farish-street-project/ for an example of students collaborating with community partners to tell a story. Lead and inspire conversations on campus and in the community with your work through exhibitions, panel discussions, film screenings, etc. Qualifications Master of Fine Arts degree related to visual or audio storytelling, documentary studies, or other relevant fields. Teaching or instructional experience in lens-based media, audio production, documentary studies, or multimedia storytelling. Ability to work as a team member responsible for multiple duties in collaborative projects. How to Apply Interested candidates should apply online at https://careers.olemiss.edu . The online application should include: 1) curriculum vitae 2) cover letter indicating documentary and teaching experience and interest 3) samples of creative work including at least one completed project that identifies your role, and 4) contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled or until an adequate applicant pool is established. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: . Education Qualifications: MFA Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Assistant-Professor-of-Practice_JR103283": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Practice in Documentary Expression",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nDepartment: About The Center for the Study of Southern Culture Founded in 1977, the CSSC defines its mission as investigating, documenting, interpreting, and teaching about the U.S. South through academic inquiry and publications, documentary studies of film, photography, and oral history, and public outreach programs. The cross-disciplinary Southern Studies faculty and the undergraduate and graduate degree programs (MA and MFA) are the core of the Center’s work. Its curricula incorporate traditional disciplines of the arts, humanities, and social sciences to form an interdisciplinary framework for studying the South. The Center promotes research on the region’s varied cultures by supporting the work of its faculty, staff, students, alumni, and visiting scholars. For more information: https://olemiss.edu/southernstudies/ Job Summary: The Southern Documentary Project (southdocs.org) is looking for an Assistant Professor of Practice in Documentary Expression. This position will help steward accountable documentary storytelling in its many forms and mediums through production, collaboration, and education. We welcome applications from critical storytellers who create work through the lens of informed practices and ethics. We encourage makers who honor lived experiences and models of cocreation in their media practice. SouthDocs is an institute of the Center for the Study of Southern Culture (https://olemiss.edu/southernstudies/) whose mission is to tell stories from the U.S. South, increase civic and cultural engagement, and train the next generation of storytellers enrolled in the Center’s academic programs. In addition to teaching and mentoring students, this person will actively contribute to the Center’s mission through creating original and collaborative work. This permanent nine-month, non-tenure track academic position includes a 2+2 teaching load with a clear path for advancement and promotion. What We Value With this hire, SouthDocs hopes to deepen our commitment to the diversity of our region. As part of an institution with a difficult history in regards to access, we take seriously our unique obligation to educate and lead the state with a commitment toward the goal of complicating our shared narratives. Job Description: Job Responsibilities 2+2 teaching load with advising responsibilities Work directly with students in the classroom and in the field. See examples of student work at https://southdocs.org/showcase/ Create original documentary projects (individually and collaboratively) utilizing photography, audio, oral history, video, or mixed media. See examples of our work at http://southdocs.org/ . Ensure accountability for and ethical representation of people and places you choose to document. Design and present technical and theory-based workshops or classes and provide constructive feedback related to documentary storytelling and related media. Engage community partners and build long-term relationships through impact-driven storytelling and programs. See https://southdocs.org/project/movementstartshere/ for one recent SouthDocs example and https://www.southernfoodways.org/features/the-farish-street-project/ for an example of students collaborating with community partners to tell a story. Lead and inspire conversations on campus and in the community with your work through exhibitions, panel discussions, film screenings, etc. Qualifications Master of Fine Arts degree related to visual or audio storytelling, documentary studies, or other relevant fields. Teaching or instructional experience in lens-based media, audio production, documentary studies, or multimedia storytelling. Ability to work as a team member responsible for multiple duties in collaborative projects. How to Apply Interested candidates should apply online at https://careers.olemiss.edu . The online application should include: 1) curriculum vitae 2) cover letter indicating documentary and teaching experience and interest 3) samples of creative work including at least one completed project that identifies your role, and 4) contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled or until an adequate applicant pool is established. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: . Education Qualifications: MFA Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Assistant-or-Associate-Professor-of-Educational-Leadership_JR103282": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Educational Leadership",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Job Summary: The University of Mississippi, Department of Leadership and Counselor Education, seeks applicants to fill a tenure-track faculty position in the Educational Leadership Program at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. Job Description: The Educational Leadership program provides a M.Ed. degree program, an Ed.S. degree program, an Ed.D. program, and a Ph.D. program. The Educational Leadership Program currently serves over 150 students. These degree programs focus on building and district PK-12 Leadership. Programs are offered on the Oxford campus, regional campuses and in an online format designed for Mississippi educators with limited access to face-to-face graduate programs. The Ph.D. degree is focused on research and scholarship; and, a new Ed.D. degree focused on practitioner preparation is based on the Carnegie Project on the Education Doctorate (CPED) framework directed toward social justice in professional practice. This is a tenure-track 9-month appointment involving teaching, research, and service to the university and profession. The applicant should be able draw on a background in educational research, and school and/or district level leadership to teach a variety courses in the preparation of K-12 school leaders. Job Responsibilities: •Teach a variety of graduate-level educational leadership courses in PK-12 leadership or related areas. •Serve as a member of doctoral comprehensive examination and dissertation committees. •Advise Educational Leadership master's and doctoral students. •Participate collegially in program governance, administration, curriculum development, and collaborative initiatives with local schools and districts. •Provide service to the University of Mississippi and community. •Demonstrate a robust research agenda and engage in professional activities and associations. Position Details Appointment: 9 Month Assignment Type: Tenure Track Minimum Qualifications •Earned doctorate in educational leadership or related field, or ABD with strong evidence of completion of earned doctorate prior to date of employment. •Active participation and potential for leadership in professional organizations related to PK-12 leadership. •Demonstrated potential to build and maintain an active research agenda associated with PK-12 leadership. •A background that demonstrates success in PK-12 administrative or teacher leadership practice, or an established research record related to PK-12 leadership. Application Procedures Qualified applicants should submit: A letter of application detailing qualification for the position, + Curriculum vitae, Names and contact information of five professional references. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled or until an adequate applicant pool is established. Questions pertaining to the position or required qualifications may be directed to Dr. Mark Deschaine, search committee chair, at 662-915-2198 or medescha@olemiss.edu . About the University of MS & Oxford, MS Founded in 1848, the University of Mississippi (UM),  affectionately known to alumni, students and friends as Ole Miss, is Mississippi's flagship university. Included in the elite group of R-1: Doctoral Universities - Highest Research Activity by the Carnegie Classification, it has a long history of producing leaders in public service, academics and business. The University of Mississippi, consistently named by The Chronicle of Higher Education as a \"Great College to Work For,\" is located in Oxford, MS, which is ranked one of the ''Top 10 Best College Towns.\" With more than 24,000 students, UM is the state's largest university and is ranked among the nation's fastest-growing institutions. The University of Mississippi, which has aggressively implemented many health and wellness initiatives for its more than 2,900 employees, has consistently been named one of Mississippi's Healthiest Workplaces. Touted as the \"Cultural Mecca of the South\", creativity abounds in Oxford as musicians, artists and writers alike find inspiration in Oxford's rich history, small town charm and creative community. Oxford is a one-hour drive south of Memphis, TN and is known as the home of Nobel Prize winning author William Faulkner. Over the years Oxford has also been known for offering exceptional culinary experiences and as the home of the University of Mississippi and the Ole Miss Rebels, there is always something here to immerse yourself in. Oxford has also been featured as a literary and arts destination in such publications as The New York Times, Southern Living, Conde Nast Traveler, and GQ. Among other cultural activities, annual events include the Oxford Film Festival, a thriving local music scene, and the Ford Center Performing Arts Series. Oxford is a vibrant university town, filled with unique shops and galleries, eclectic restaurants and clubs, historic landmarks, and comfortable inns. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: •Teach a variety of graduate-level educational leadership courses in PK-12 leadership or related areas. •Serve as a member of doctoral comprehensive examination and dissertation committees. •Advise Educational Leadership master's and doctoral students. •Participate collegially in program governance, administration, curriculum development, and collaborative initiatives with local schools and districts. •Provide service to the University of Mississippi and community. •Demonstrate a robust research agenda and engage in professional activities and associations. Education Qualifications: PhD: Educational Leadership Experience: Related Experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Assistant-Professor-of-Economics_JR103316": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Economics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\nFull time\n\nDepartment: The University of Mississippi ’ s Department of Economics invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor position beginning August 2027. Job Summary: Performs the functions of teaching, research or creative activities, and service and are assigned to an academic unit appropriate for their Discipline descriptor. Job Description: Tenure Track Faculty members perform the functions of teaching, research or creative activities, and service and are assigned to an academic unit appropriate for their Discipline descriptor. Tenure Track Faculty members are usually hired as assistant professors and enter the tenure track. For example, an assistant professor must serve a probationary period of five years and be reviewed for tenure during his or her sixth year of continuous employment at the University. Tenure Track Faculty members may be hired, particularly at the associate and full ranks, with a reduction in the tenure probationary period, as explained in the Tenure Policy. Tenure Track Faculty members at the Associate and Full ranks are full members of the Graduate Faculty, while those at the Assistant rank are Associate Members of the Graduate Faculty. The Department has a need for an economist who specializes in Monetary Economics and who can teach at both the graduate and the undergraduate levels. The candidate will be expected to contribute to our undergraduate emphasis in Financial Economics and teach in our graduate macroeconomics sequence. The ability to teach time series at the graduate level is a plus, but not required. Applicants must provide a cover letter, a curriculum vita, one working paper, a graduate transcript, and three letters of recommendation. The cover letter should describe current and projected research and teaching interests. A Ph.D. completed by August 2027 is expected. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: . Education Qualifications: PhD (Required) Experience: Related Experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Instructor---Mechanical-Engineering_JR103137-1": {
      "title": "Instructor - Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Mechanical Engineering Job Summary: Performs the function of teaching with renewable contracts. Not eligible for tenure. Job Description: Position Description The Department of Mechanical Engineering at the University of Mississippi (Ole Miss), Oxford, Mississippi, invites applications for a full-time Instructor of Mechanical Engineering. This teaching-focused, non-tenure-track faculty appointment will strengthen hands-on undergraduate instruction in mechanical engineering laboratories and related engineering courses. The successful candidate will teach and support hands-on undergraduate laboratories, including structures and dynamics, materials, thermal-fluids, instrumentation, design, or related mechanical engineering laboratory experiences. The Instructor will work closely with faculty course coordinators, laboratory faculty directors, department staff, and the machine shop supervisor to support safe, effective, well-maintained, and practice-oriented instructional laboratories. Primary Responsibilities Teach, coordinate, or support hands-on undergraduate mechanical engineering laboratories, with possible assignments in Structures and Dynamics Lab, Materials Lab, Thermal-Fluids Lab, instrumentation, design, or related laboratory courses. Teach undergraduate mechanical engineering or engineering science courses as assigned, based on departmental needs and the candidate's expertise. Develop, revise, and assess laboratory manuals, experiments, safety procedures, demonstrations, project-based activities, and course materials. Work collaboratively with faculty laboratory directors, course coordinators, department staff, and the machine shop supervisor to support laboratory safety, equipment readiness, maintenance coordination, and continuous improvement. Mentor undergraduate students in laboratory practice, engineering problem solving, professional conduct, and safe use of equipment and facilities. Contribute to ABET-related assessment, curriculum coordination, laboratory documentation, department service, and other continuous-improvement activities. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Required Qualifications Master’s degree in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related engineering field by the appointment start date. Relevant laboratory, instructional, technical, or hands-on engineering experience. Demonstrated ability or strong potential to teach and supervise hands-on undergraduate engineering laboratory experiences. Strong written and oral communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and students. Commitment to safe, student-centered, inclusive, and professionally rigorous engineering education, as demonstrated through teaching, mentoring, laboratory, professional, or related experience. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related engineering field. Experience teaching, coordinating, or supporting undergraduate laboratories in structures and dynamics, materials, thermal-fluid sciences, instrumentation, design, manufacturing, or related mechanical engineering areas. Experience developing or modernizing laboratory experiments, safety documentation, assessment tools, lab manuals, or project-based learning activities. Experience working with machine shop, fabrication, instrumentation, testing, or mechanical systems in an instructional, industrial, or applied engineering setting. Evidence of student-centered teaching, undergraduate mentoring, course assessment, ABET-related documentation, or structured curriculum improvement. Application Procedures Application materials should include: A cover letter addressing qualifications, laboratory teaching interests, industry or professional engineering experience, and fit with the department's instructional needs. A current curriculum vitae. A statement describing laboratory teaching experience, instructional approach, and experience with hands-on, project-based, safety-focused, or practice-oriented engineering education. Names and contact information for three professional references. Questions regarding the position may be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Damian Stoddard, at dlstodda@olemiss.edu. Education Qualifications: Master's (Required) Experience: Related Experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Assistant-Associate-Professor-in-Physics-Education-Research_JR103118": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Physics Education Research",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-18\nFull time\n\nDepartment: The successful candidate will be a regular faculty member in Physics and Astronomy. Job Summary: The Department of Physics and Astronomy at the University of Mississippi seeks highly qualified candidates for a tenure-track faculty position in physics education research. The role involves teaching (undergraduate and graduate courses), research (including supervision of students, especially in projects leading to master’s and doctoral degrees), and service (to the department, university, community, profession). Job Description: The Department of Physics and Astronomy at the University of Mississippi (UM) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in physics education research (PER) at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. This position is supported in part by an endowment to honor F. Douglas and Cora Beal Shields and represents a significant institutional investment in establishing PER as a strategic area of excellence.   The purpose of the endowment is to recruit and retain outstanding faculty for the Department of Physics and Astronomy; to advance the understanding of physics pedagogy and contribute to the field of PER; to raise the quality of instruction across the department; and to seed a new research area with meaningful doctoral production. This position offers a unique opportunity to establish a nationally visible PER group at a flagship R1 university with strong undergraduate and graduate physics programs and to help define the future direction of PER at the University of Mississippi.   The position includes a competitive salary and a startup package. Income from the endowment will be used to provide funds for research and creative activity, including but not limited to support for personnel (graduate teaching and research assistants, undergraduate learning assistants), operational support for scientific studies, travel to conferences, and hosting visitors and workshops. There are also opportunities for collaboration and support in connection with UM’s Center for Excellence in Teaching and Learning, Center for Math and Science Education, Center for STEM Learning, and Institute for Data Science.   The Physics and Astronomy faculty are eager to attract a driven, energetic colleague who can help to craft a long-term vision for growing out PER in the department. Ideally, the new faculty member will be a proselytizer for active learning and a catalyst for extending modern, evidence-based practices. Like all our tenure-track faculty, this person must also excel at mentoring graduate students and providing them with research opportunities that lead to the Ph.D. degree.   The search committee’s aperture for evaluating the candidates is quite wide. Applications are encouraged from researchers working in all areas of PER and at all career stages. The department is open to recruiting an emerging scholar or an established researcher who could be hired with an accelerated tenure clock.   A Ph.D. in Physics or a closely related field is required. Candidates should have relevant experience beyond the doctoral degree, a strong record of publication or impact in the field, demonstrated potential for establishing and sustaining a nationally recognized research program, and well-conceived plans to pursue external funding opportunities. The successful candidate is expected to establish an active and independent research program, teach effectively at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, and contribute to the service missions of the department and university.   Applications should be submitted online via the Faculty Career Site portal, which is accessible from https://olemiss.edu/hr/um-careers/ by clicking through to External Applicants > Faculty Openings. A complete application comprises a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement (up to 2 pages), research statement (up to 5 pages), and three (3) confidential letters of recommendation. Evaluation of applicants will begin on Friday, November 6 and continue until the position is filled. Questions should be sent to persearch@phy.olemiss.edu . Applicants’ recommenders can be directed to submit their letters to that same email address.   –––   Founded in 1848, the University of Mississippi (UM), affectionately known to alumni, students and friends as Ole Miss, is Mississippi's flagship university. Included in the elite group of “R1: Very High Research Spending and Doctorate Production” by the Carnegie Classification, it has a long history of producing leaders in public service, academics, and business. The University of Mississippi, consistently named by The Chronicle of Higher Education as a “Great College to Work For,” is located in Oxford, MS, which is ranked one of the “Top 10 Best College Towns.” With more than 24,000 students, UM is the state's largest university and is ranked among the nation's fastest-growing institutions. The University of Mississippi, which has aggressively implemented many health and wellness initiatives for its more than 2,900 employees, has consistently been named one of Mississippi’s Healthiest Workplaces.   Touted as the “Cultural Mecca of the South,” creativity abounds in Oxford as musicians, artists, and writers alike find inspiration in Oxford’s rich history, small-town charm and creative community. Oxford is a one-hour drive south of Memphis, TN and is known as the home of Nobel Prize winning author William Faulkner. Over the years Oxford has also been known for offering exceptional culinary experiences and as the home of the University of Mississippi and the Ole Miss Rebels, there is always something here to immerse yourself in. Oxford has also been featured as a literary and arts destination in such publications as The New York Times, Southern Living, Condé Nast Traveler, and GQ. Among other cultural activities, annual events include the Oxford Film Festival, a thriving local music scene, and the Ford Center Performing Arts Series. Oxford is a vibrant university town, filled with unique shops and galleries, eclectic restaurants and clubs, historic landmarks, and comfortable inns.   The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, national origin, ethnicity, citizenship, age, disability, military status, protected veteran status, genetic information, or any other legally protected status. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Teach a standard load of undergraduate and graduate physics courses. Build and sustain an active, high-impact research program in Physics Education Research that publishes regularly in relevant scientific journals, involves undergraduate and graduate students, and leads to significant graduate degree production. Contribute to departmental and university committees, engage in public outreach activities, and/or provide service to the profession. Education Qualifications: PhD: Physics (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Tenure-Track-Faculty-Position-in-Organic-Organometallic-Synthesis_JR102983": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Faculty Position in Organic/Organometallic Synthesis",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-18\nFull time\n\nDepartment: The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry at the University of Mississippi is a vibrant and growing unit with excellent research infrastructure, including modern instrumentation and core facilities. Ole Miss offers a highly collaborative research environment and strong institutional support for new faculty. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until an adequate applicant pool is established. For full consideration, applicants are encouraged to submit their materials by October 15, 2026. Job Summary: The successful candidate will develop and maintain a vigorous, externally funded research program in chemistry, teach undergraduate and graduate courses in the Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry, mentor undergraduate and graduate students, and contribute service to the department, college, university, and chemistry-discipline. Job Description: The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry at the University of Mississippi invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor starting in August 2027. We seek an outstanding organic/organometallic synthetic chemist who will establish a vigorous, externally funded research program, excel in teaching at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, and contribute to departmental, college, and university service. Individuals with expertise in any area of organic or organometallic chemistry, especially with research interests in synthetic chemistry of macromolecules and/or green/sustainable chemistry, and in those areas complementing active research programs in the Department, are strongly encouraged to apply. Applicants must have a Ph.D. and relevant postdoctoral experience in synthetic chemistry or a closely related field. The Department has 17 tenured and tenure-track faculty, 10 teaching faculty, and a vibrant graduate Ph.D. program. Construction of a new $175M STEM education facility was completed in 2024 fostering interdisciplinary opportunities to fulfill our commitment to excellence in chemical education. The successful candidate is expected to establish a nationally recognized, externally-funded research program; provide mentorship for graduate and undergraduate researchers; demonstrate excellence in teaching; and perform service to the department, college, university, and community ( https://visitoxfordms.com/ ). Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until an adequate applicant pool is established. Applications must be submitted online (careers.olemiss.edu) and should include a cover letter, a current CV, research plans, and a teaching philosophy statement. During the online application process, applicants will be prompted to provide the names and email addresses of three professional references that will provide letters of recommendation. The University of Mississippi has a Carnegie classification of R1 and is rated a “Great Colleges to Work For” by the Chronicle of Higher Education. The University of Mississippi is an EOE / AA / Minority / Female / Vet / Disability / Sexual Orientation / Gender Identity / Title VI / Title VII / Title IX / 504 / ADA / ADEA employer. The successful candidate will be expected to: Develop and maintain a strong, independent research program that attracts external funding and produces high-quality scholarly publications. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in organic chemistry, including both lecture and laboratory components. Actively mentor and supervise undergraduate and graduate students in research. Contribute to service activities that support the mission of the department, university, and profession. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Establish and lead an independent, externally funded research program that produces high-impact scholarly publications and advances the field of chemistry or biochemistry. Teach assigned undergraduate and graduate courses (lecture and laboratory), develop new courses as needed, and incorporate modern pedagogical practices. Recruit, mentor, and supervise undergraduate research students, M.S. students, and Ph.D. students, including serving on thesis and dissertation committees. Actively seek and secure external research funding from federal agencies (e.g., NSF, NIH, DOE), foundations, and other sources. Participate in departmental governance, including serving on committees related to curriculum, instrumentation, safety, graduate admissions, and faculty searches. Contribute to college and university service through participation in broader academic committees and initiatives. Engage in professional service, such as reviewing manuscripts, grant proposals, and serving on conference or professional society committees. Education Qualifications: PhD: Chemistry (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Assistant--Associate--or-Full-Clinical-Professor-of-Law--tenure-track-or-non-tenure-track---Director-of-the-Immigration-Clinic_JR103076": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Clinical Professor of Law (tenure-track or non-tenure-track), Director of the Immigration Clinic",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-07\nFull time\n\nDepartment: School of Law Job Summary: Assistant, Associate, or Full Clinical Professor of Law (tenure-track or non-tenure-track), Director of the Immigration Clinic The University of Mississippi School of Law invites applicants with a passion for immigration law, including both teaching and representing live clients with immigration-associated legal issues, to establish and direct an Immigration Clinic. The Clinic’s mission will be to train students to protect the rights of both our local and state-wide immigrant community, and to engage in public service lawyering in the immigration policy field. Job Description: The Director’s status may either be tenure-track or long-term contract. The Director and the Law School Dean will reach a decision based upon the Director’s desire, the Clinc’s specific needs, as well as the Law School’s administrative and pedagogical needs. The position will be a twelve-month appointment. The Director’s primary responsibilities will include managing caseload, selecting cases, litigating cases, supervising students, and administering all aspects of the Clinic, including managing the Clinic budget and supervising Clinic staff. The successful candidate for Director will achieve excellence in some or all of the following, depending on the specific scope of contractual employment: scholarship, teaching, public advocacy, and service to the Law School and community consistent with his or her clinical responsibilities. We seek applicants with demonstrated accomplishments in immigration-related litigation, including some or all of the following: representing immigrants in removal proceedings, immigration-related complex federal litigation, representing asylees in asylum cases, and contributions to immigration policy and community education projects within their local communities. The Director should be prepared to partnerwith local, regional, and national civil rights and immigration-focused organizations, many of which are currently working in local and state immigrant communities in Mississippi. Applicants should have a minimum of five years of legal experience, which should include litigating within the areas of law referenced above. The Director should also possess staff management experience, junior attorney mentorship experience, or their equivalents. The Director must hold a J.D. degree from an American Bar Association-accredited law school or international equivalent. The Director must be admitted to the Mississippi Bar, obtain admission through reciprocity with his or her current state of licensure, or obtain admission through examination at the earliest practicable administration of the Mississippi Bar Examination. The successful candidate will demonstrate promise as a teacher, a commitment to clinical teaching and public interest law practice, and service. Any candidate pursuing a tenure-track appointment should have a record of scholarship or evidence of promise to produce scholarship that is consistent with the demands of clinical teaching and litigation. Applicants will be accepted until the position is filled or an adequate applicant pool is established. Interested persons must apply online at careers.olemiss.edu by submitting a cover letter, resume or curriculum vitae, and a list of at least three references. Direct applications are welcome and should be sent to Professor Martin Edwards, Chair of the Faculty Appointments Committee, at amedwar1@olemiss.edu. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Duties as assigned Education Qualifications: JD (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Assistant--Associate--or-Full-Professor-1_JR103051": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-07\nFull time\n\nDepartment: School of Law Job Summary: The University of Mississippi School of Law invites applications and nominations for as many as two tenure-track professors to begin in the 2027-2028 academic year. Appointments will be at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor depending on experience. We invite and encourage scholars in all fields to apply as our teaching needs are quite broad. We are most interested in candidates who wish to teach Torts or Civil Procedure. We are also interested in candidates who wish to teach other 1L and Bar-tested subjects such as Criminal Law and Family Law, and we may also consider candidates who wish to teach Criminal Procedure, Evidence, and Business and Commercial Law. Finally, we are always looking for candidates who can expand our course offerings in growing practice areas such as Health Law, Labor & Employment Law, and Insurance Law. Position Details Appointment: 9-Month Assignment Type: Tenure-Track Job Description: Tenure Track Faculty members perform the functions of teaching, research or creative activities, and service and are assigned to an academic unit appropriate for their Discipline descriptor. Tenure Track Faculty members are usually hired as assistant professors and enter the tenure track. For example, an assistant professor must serve a probationary period of five years and be reviewed for tenure during his or her sixth year of continuous employment at the University. Tenure Track Faculty members may be hired, particularly at the associate and full ranks, with a reduction in the tenure probationary period, as explained in the Tenure Policy. Tenure Track Faculty members at the Associate and Full ranks are full members of the Graduate Faculty, while those at the Assistant rank are Associate Members of the Graduate Faculty. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Qualifications: A candidate must have a J.D. degree from an ABA accredited law school, or equivalent international degree, and a commitment to teaching in an environment dedicated to excellence in teaching and mentoring of students. All applicants should have a distinguished academic background, and either demonstrated promise or a record of excellence in both scholarship and teaching. The most competitive candidates will have a record of published or accepted law review articles or equivalents and some record demonstrating potential for excellence in teaching. Application Instructions: To apply, please submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and contact information for three references with your online application. We also welcome, but do not require, additional materials such as a research agenda or teaching statement. The position will be open until filled or an adequate applicant pool is reached. Questions are welcome, and should be directed to Professor Martin Edwards, Chair of the Faculty Appointments Committee, at amedwar1@olemiss.edu. Education Qualifications: JD (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Frank-R-Day-Mississippi-Bankers-Association-Endowed-Chair_JR102985": {
      "title": "Frank R. Day/Mississippi Bankers Association Endowed Chair",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-28\nFull time\n\nDepartment: School of Business Administration- Department of Finance Job Summary: Performs the functions of teaching, research or creative activities, and service in the Department of Finance. Job Description: The Department of Finance in the School of Business Administration at The University of Mississippi invites applications for the Frank R. Day/Mississippi Bankers Association Endowed Chair. This position is at the rank of Associate or Full Professor. The appointment can be either tenured or non-tenure track and will start in August 2027. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate banking and finance courses and carry out duties assigned by the Dean of the School of Business Administration and the Chair of the Department of Finance. The standard teaching load for the tenured position is 2/1; the non-tenure track load is 3/3. Summer teaching opportunities are available for additional compensation, subject to course and funding availability. Tenure requires a strong record of both research and teaching. This chair carries significant service responsibilities that distinguish it from a traditional faculty appointment. The holder will serve as Director of the Mississippi School of Banking, which is a professional development program for banking practitioners, and will maintain an active relationship with the Mississippi Bankers Association. Additional responsibilities include managing the Banking Symposium, a Banking Symposium that regularly draws 200 or more attendees, along with other initiatives that elevate the banking industry's profile in Mississippi, the region, and nationally. To be considered, applicants must have earned a doctorate degree in finance from an AACSB accredited or well-regarded business program. Candidates must have demonstrated research and teaching interests in banking (finance). Candidates for a tenured position should possess a proven track record of original research and/or publication in top banking and finance journals. Candidates for a non-tenure track position must be academically qualified to teach banking and finance courses under AACSB accreditation standards. All candidates must provide evidence of a strong commitment to effective teaching and service. No candidate will be considered who does not meet the departmental AACSB standards for Scholarly Academics (SA). Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: The successful candidate will teach undergraduate banking and finance courses and carry out duties assigned by the Dean of the School of Business Administration and the Chair of the Department of Finance. The standard teaching load for the tenured position is 2/1; the non-tenure track load is 3/3. Summer teaching opportunities are available for additional compensation, subject to course and funding availability. Tenure requires a strong record of both research and teaching.   This chair carries significant service responsibilities that distinguish it from a traditional faculty appointment. The holder will serve as Director of the Mississippi School of Banking, which is a professional development program for banking practitioners, and will maintain an active relationship with the Mississippi Bankers Association. Additional responsibilities include managing the Banking Symposium, a Banking Symposium that regularly draws 200 or more attendees, along with other initiatives that elevate the banking industry's profile in Mississippi, the region, and nationally. Qualifications To be considered, applicants must have earned a doctorate degree in finance from an AACSB accredited or well-regarded business program. Candidates must have demonstrated research and teaching interests in banking (finance). Candidates for a tenured position should possess a proven track record of original research and/or publication in top banking and finance journals. Candidates for a non-tenure track position must be academically qualified to teach banking and finance courses under AACSB accreditation standards. All candidates must provide evidence of a strong commitment to effective teaching and service. No candidate will be considered who does not meet the departmental AACSB standards for Scholarly Academics (SA). Screening of applications will begin immediately. The position will be open until filled or until an adequate applicant pool is achieved. Apply online (only) at https://careers.olemiss.edu . Applications cannot be accepted in any other format. Candidates should submit a curriculum vitae, cover letter, and evidence of effective teaching. At least three references will be required from candidates invited to campus. For additional information, contact the search committee chair, Dr. Robert Van Ness, 329 Holman Hall, School of Business Administration, PO Box 1848, The University of Mississippi, University, MS 38677-1848 (rvanness@bus.olemiss.edu). Education Qualifications: PhD: Banking & Finance (Required) Experience: Relevant experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
    "https://olemiss.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External__Faculty/job/University-MS/Instructional-Asst-Professor---Mechanical-Engineering_JR102964": {
      "title": "Instructional Asst Professor - Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nFull time\n\nDepartment: Mechanical Engineering Job Summary: The Department of Mechanical Engineering at the University of Mississippi (Ole Miss), Oxford, Mississippi, invites applications for a full-time Instructional Assistant Professor of Mechanical Engineering. This teaching-focused, non-tenure-track faculty appointment will strengthen high-quality undergraduate instruction in mechanical engineering courses. Candidates from all areas of mechanical engineering will be considered. Candidates with expertise and teaching capacity in core undergraduate areas such as materials, mechanics, dynamics, thermal-fluid sciences, design, engineering systems, hands-on laboratories, or related mechanical engineering topics are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will join a collegial department committed to rigorous engineering education, student success, laboratory safety, continuous improvement, and preparing graduates for professional practice and advanced study. Job Description: Teach undergraduate mechanical engineering and engineering science courses, with assignments based on departmental needs and the candidate's expertise. Teach, coordinate, or support hands-on undergraduate laboratories, potentially including structures and dynamics, materials, thermal-fluids, instrumentation, design, or related laboratory experiences. Develop, revise, and assess course and laboratory materials that support active learning, project-based learning, engineering practice, and strong student outcomes. Work collaboratively with faculty course coordinators, laboratory faculty directors, department staff, and the machine shop supervisor to support safe, effective, and well-maintained instructional laboratories. Mentor undergraduate students, maintain accessible office hours, and contribute to student success, retention, and professional development activities. Participate in departmental service, curriculum coordination, ABET-related assessment and documentation, and other continuous-improvement activities. Engage in professional, scholarly, educational, or industry-facing activities that enrich teaching and advance the department's instructional mission, as appropriate for the appointment. Join the Ole Miss Family — Build Your Legacy Here! Job Responsibilities: Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related engineering discipline by the appointment start date. Demonstrated potential for, or evidence of, effective undergraduate teaching in mechanical engineering or related engineering science areas. Ability to teach core undergraduate mechanical engineering or engineering science courses and to support hands-on or project-based learning. Strong written and oral communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and students. Commitment to safe, student-centered, inclusive, and professionally rigorous engineering education, as demonstrated through teaching, mentoring, laboratory, professional, or related experience. Preferred Qualifications Two or more years of teaching, laboratory teaching, instructional support, industry, or closely related professional engineering experience. Experience teaching or supporting courses in materials science, mechanics of materials, dynamics, thermal-fluid sciences, design, engineering systems, instrumentation, or related mechanical engineering topics. Experience developing, modernizing, or coordinating hands-on laboratories, including attention to safety, equipment readiness, assessment, and coordination with shop or technical staff. Evidence of student-centered teaching practices, active learning, project-based learning, or effective instruction in high-enrollment undergraduate engineering courses. Experience with course assessment, ABET-related documentation, curriculum improvement, or other structured continuous-improvement processes. Professional, industry, or research experience that connects mechanical engineering fundamentals to contemporary engineering practice. Application Procedures Application materials should include: A cover letter addressing qualifications, teaching interests, and fit with the department's instructional needs. A current curriculum vitae. A teaching statement describing undergraduate teaching experience, teaching philosophy, and experience with hands-on, laboratory, project-based, or practice-oriented engineering education. Names and contact information for three professional references. Questions regarding the position may be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Samrat Choudhury, at schoudhu@olemiss.edu. Education Qualifications: PhD (Required) Experience: Related Experience Interview Requirement: Interview Requirements: Any candidate who is called for an interview must notify the Department of Equal Opportunity/Regulatory Compliance in writing of any reasonable accommodation needed prior to the date of the interview. EEO Statement: The University of Mississippi provides equal opportunity in any employment practice, education program, or education activity to all qualified persons. The University complies with all applicable laws regarding equal opportunity and affirmative action and does not unlawfully discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment based upon race, color, gender, sex, pregnancy, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, citizenship, national origin, age, disability, veteran status, or genetic information. Background Check Statement: The University of Mississippi is committed to providing a safe campus community. UM conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check."
    },
